<title>USA: U.S. Dec consumer prices rose 0.3 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Dec consumer prices rose 0.3 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-01-14</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Labor Department monthly Consumer Price Index (CPI-U), 1982-84 equals 100:</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:  Seasonally Adj   Unadjusted</p>
<p>    -			   Dec    Nov   Dec96/95</p>
<p> All Items		    0.3    0.3	3.3</p>
<p> -Excluding Food/Energy   0.1    0.2	2.6</p>
<p> Energy			 1.6    1.2	8.6</p>
<p> Food, Drink		  0.1    0.3	4.2</p>
<p> Food			   0.1    0.3	4.3</p>
<p> CPI-Urban Consumers-X  158.6  158.6	N/A</p>
<p>X-Data unadjusted. N/A-Not Available.  </p>
<p>    Percent Changes:  Seasonally Adj   Unadjusted</p>
<p>    -			   Dec    Nov   Dec96/95</p>
<p> Services		     0.2    0.2	3.3</p>
<p> Housing			0.3    0.2	2.9</p>
<p> -Home Fuel Oil	     3.1    2.0     23.1</p>
<p> -Homeowner Costs-Y	 0.2    0.3	2.8</p>
<p> Apparel, Upkeep	    0.2    0.5     -0.2</p>
<p> Transportation	     0.6    0.2	4.4</p>
<p> -Gasoline		    3.1    1.8     12.4</p>
<p> -New Cars		    0.1   -0.4	1.6</p>
<p>Y-Dec 1982=100 base.  </p>
<p>    Percent Changes:  Seasonally Adj   Unadjusted</p>
<p>    -			   Dec    Nov   Dec96/95</p>
<p> Medical Care		 0.2    0.3	3.0</p>
<p> Commodities		  0.3    0.3	3.2</p>
<p> CPI-W			  0.3    0.3	3.3</p>
<p>CPI-W is the Consumer Price Index for urban wage earners and clerical workers.  </p>
<p>Wall Street economists expected a 0.3 percent rise in December consumer prices. Excluding food and energy, they expected a 0.2 percent increase.</p>
<p>The 3.3 percent rise in the CPI for 1996 compared to a 2.5 percent annual rate for 1995. It was the largest gain since a 6.1 percent rise in 1990.</p>
<p>Also, the 2.6 percent rate for consumer prices excluding food and energy in 1996 compared to a 3.0 percent increase in 1995. The 1996 core rate was the lowest since 2.6 percent in 1994.  </p>
<p>The CPI energy component rose by 8.6 percent in 1996 following a 1.3 percent drop in 1995. The 1996 energy increase was the largest since 18.1 percent in 1990.</p>
<p>Labor said airline fares rose 3.9 percent in December.</p>
</text>
